---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const ARTICLE_META = {
  title: "What Is Distributed Tracing? A Complete Guide (2026)",
  author: "The 24Observe Engineering Team",
  date: "2026-09-16",
  readTime: "23 min read",
  category: "Monitoring & Observability",
  description: "Distributed tracing explained in plain English. Learn what spans, trace IDs, and context propagation mean, why microservices broke traditional debugging, and how modern teams use tracing to find the one slow service out of fifty in seconds instead of hours.",
  slug: "what-is-distributed-tracing-complete-guide",
};

const FEATURED_GUIDES = [
  { title: "Best PagerDuty Alternatives in 2026", link: "/blog/best-pagerduty-alternatives-2026" },
  { title: "What Is a SIEM? (2026 Guide)", link: "/blog/what-is-a-siem-complete-guide" },
  { title: "What Is MTTR? (2026 Guide)", link: "/blog/what-is-mttr-how-to-measure-and-reduce" },
  { title: "API Monitoring Complete Guide (2026)", link: "/blog/api-monitoring-complete-guide" },
  { title: "What Is AI Observability? (2026 Guide)", link: "/blog/what-is-ai-observability-complete-guide" },
  { title: "AI Agent Observability: How to Monitor", link: "/blog/ai-agent-observability-how-to-monitor" },
  { title: "SIEM vs Observability: Differences & Convergence", link: "/blog/siem-vs-observability-differences-overlap-converging" },
];

const PLATFORM_SPECIALS = [
  { title: "August 2026 Release Notes", link: "/blog#releases" },
  { title: "June 2026 Release Notes", link: "/blog#releases" },
  { title: "May 2026 (Phase 2 — Agent API)", link: "/blog#releases" },
  { title: "AI Agent API & Open API", link: "/docs/api-for-agents" },
  { title: "MCP Server Setup Guide", link: "/docs/mcp" },
  { title: "Self-Host Air-Gapped Setup", link: "/self-host" },
];

const FAQS = [
  {
    q: "Is distributed tracing the same thing as logging?",
    a: "No, though they work well together. A log is a record of a discrete event from one service's own point of view. A trace connects records across many services into a single timeline for one specific request, tracking how that request moved and how long it spent at each stop. Many mature setups embed the trace ID inside log lines specifically so the two can be correlated later."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need microservices before distributed tracing is worth adopting?",
    a: "Mostly, yes, in the sense that its value scales directly with how many separate components a typical request touches. A single monolithic application running on one server gets comparatively little extra value from tracing, since a stack trace and solid logging already cover most of the same ground. The moment a request starts crossing network boundaries into two or more independently deployed components, tracing starts paying for itself quickly."
  },
  {
    q: "What is a trace waterfall or flame graph, exactly?",
    a: "It is simply a visual representation of a trace's spans, laid out along a timeline, with each span shown as a horizontal bar whose width represents its duration, and bars nested underneath their parent span to show the call hierarchy. It is designed to be read immediately: the widest bar is almost always where the time went."
  },
  {
    q: "Does distributed tracing add meaningful performance overhead?",
    a: "Well implemented tracing adds a small, generally negligible overhead per request, mostly the cost of generating span data and exporting it asynchronously in the background rather than blocking the actual request. Poorly implemented tracing, particularly synchronous exports that block the request path, or excessive unsampled trace volume, can absolutely become a real performance problem, which is exactly why sampling and asynchronous export through something like an OpenTelemetry Collector matter."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between OpenTelemetry, OpenTracing, and OpenCensus?",
    a: "OpenTracing and OpenCensus were two separate, competing earlier efforts at standardizing telemetry instrumentation. They merged in 2019 into OpenTelemetry, which is now the single actively maintained standard. If you encounter references to OpenTracing or OpenCensus in older documentation or legacy code, treat them as predecessors that have been superseded; new instrumentation work should be built on OpenTelemetry."
  },
  {
    q: "How long should I retain trace data?",
    a: "This depends heavily on your use case and budget, but most teams retain full trace detail for a relatively short window, often somewhere between one and four weeks, since traces are primarily useful for active incident investigation and recent performance analysis rather than long term historical trend analysis, which metrics handle far more efficiently at a fraction of the storage cost."
  },
  {
    q: "Can distributed tracing help with security investigations, not just performance?",
    a: "Increasingly, yes. A trace showing exactly which services a particular request touched, in what order, and with what parameters, can be genuinely useful evidence during a security investigation, particularly for understanding the blast radius of a compromised credential or an unusual internal service call. This is part of why modern platforms increasingly treat operational tracing and SIEM security detection as feeding into the same underlying investigation rather than living in entirely separate tools."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need a dedicated tracing backend, or can I get away with just logs and metrics?",
    a: "You can go a fairly long way with well correlated logs and metrics, particularly on smaller systems, but the moment you find yourself repeatedly manually reconstructing a request's path across multiple services by cross referencing timestamps, that manual work is precisely what a proper trace automates for you, and at that point the investment starts paying for itself very quickly."
  },
];
---

<Base
  activeNav="blog"
  title={ARTICLE_META.title}
  description={ARTICLE_META.description}
>
  <main style="max-width: 100%; padding: 16px clamp(24px, 3.5vw, 64px);">

    {/* DESKTOP 3-COLUMN LAYOUT */}
    <div style="display: flex; gap: 40px; align-items: flex-start;">

      {/* LEFT SIDEBAR */}
      <aside class="desktop-sidebar" style="width: 270px; flex-shrink: 0; position: sticky; top: 80px;">
        <div style="margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="background: #3B9112; color: #ffffff; font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 800; padding: 4px 14px; border-radius: 20px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">
            FEATURED GUIDES
          </span>
        </div>
